original_source_statement_tex:
\label{lemma-C-is-faithful}
For a ring $R$, the functor $C : \text{Mod}_R \to \text{Mod}_R$ is
exact and reflects injections and surjections.

\label{lemma-split-surjection}
Let $R$ be a ring. A morphism $f: M \to N$ in $\text{Mod}_R$ is universally
injective if and only if $C(f): C(N) \to C(M)$ is a split surjection.

stage_a_standalone_statement_draft:
For a commutative ring $R$, define the contravariant $R$-module functor $C(M)=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb Z}(M,\mathbb Q/\mathbb Z)$, with $R$-action $(r\cdot\lambda)(m)=\lambda(rm)$. Let $u:M\to N$ be an $R$-linear map. The following are equivalent:
(1) for every $R$-module $P$ the map $u\otimes_R\mathrm{id}_P:M\otimes_RP\to N\otimes_RP$ is injective (that is, $u$ is universally injective);
(2) the induced map $C(u):C(N)\to C(M)$ admits an $R$-linear right inverse, i.e. is a split surjection.

